          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "United States guidance recommends routine annual influenza vaccination for everyone aged 6 months and older, unless they have a contraindication. This guidance does not restrict vaccination to high-risk groups, as described in the \"National immunization advisory committee and infectious diseases society guidance\" (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals).\n\nDespite this broad recommendation, some conditions are specifically mentioned where influenza vaccination is important:\n*   **Coronary artery disease**: Annual influenza vaccination is considered a cardiovascular measure for secondary prevention after a diagnosis of coronary artery disease, according to \"Major cardiology society consensus guidance\" (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention).\n*   **COPD**: Vaccination against influenza, along with other respiratory illnesses, can help reduce exacerbations for individuals with COPD, as noted in the \"Global respiratory strategy report\" (guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
